{#
  _own_image.j2 — the SINGLE gate on a service's `build:` block.

  Compose tags a build with the service's `image:`. A template that rendered
  `build:` beside an operator's own `services.<key>.image` would build OSPREY's
  recipe and tag it with the operator's name. So the build renders only while
  the service runs the image OSPREY builds: no `services.<key>.image` (unset or
  a null block), or one naming the packaged `osprey_images.<key>`. Any other
  image is pulled and run as named, so a deploy never builds a Dockerfile under
  a name the operator chose for something else.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IMPORTING AND WHITESPACE
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A plain (context-free) import, as for `_network_axis.j2` beside it:

      {% import "services/_own_image.j2" as own %}

  The `build:` block is the call body, opened right after the `image:` line
  and closed with a left-trim marker, so the body supplies its own leading
  newline and a skipped build leaves no blank line:

      image: ...
  {%- call own.build(svc.image, osprey_images.<key>) %}
      build:
        ...
  {%- endcall %}

  A template where several services share one image picks ONE builder instead
  (bluesky, virtual_accelerator, qmd): two builds of one tag race each other.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT AN EDIT HERE REDDENS
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Every importer's render is pinned byte for byte. Regenerate in the same
  change:

      PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv/bin/python tests/templates/test_render_defaults_golden.py
      PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv/bin/python tests/templates/test_render_axis_shapes.py
#}

{#- The call body, only while `image` is unset or names `own_image`. -#}
{%- macro build(image, own_image) -%}
{%- if not image or image == own_image -%}
{{ caller() }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}
